// ------------------------------------------------------------
// Example: Bubble sort (in-place) + printing a heap list
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Purpose:
//   Sort a list of numbers in ascending order using the bubble sort algorithm,
//   then print the sorted result (one element per line).
//
// What it demonstrates:
//   - Working with heap-backed arrays/lists created via `new[...]`
//   - Getting and setting elements with `heap_get` / `heap_set`
//   - Using `len(values)` to obtain the list length
//   - Nested `while` loops
//   - Comparisons (`>`) and conditional swapping
//   - Returning a value from a function
//   - Printing list elements via a helper function
//
// Algorithm overview (bubble sort):
//   Bubble sort repeatedly walks through the array and swaps adjacent elements
//   that are out of order. After each outer loop pass, the largest remaining
//   element has “bubbled” to the end, so the inner loop can stop one element
//   earlier each time.
//
// Complexity:
//   - Time: O(n^2) comparisons/swaps in the worst case.
//   - Space: O(1) extra space (sorts in-place).
//
// Important behavior note:
//   - This implementation sorts the input list *in-place* (it mutates `values`).
//     The function also returns the same list pointer for convenience.
//
// Expected output for the demo input [5, 1, 4, 2, 8]:
//   1
//   2
//   4
//   5
//   8
//

// Sort `values` in ascending order using bubble sort.
// `values` is expected to be a heap list pointer (e.g., created with `new[...]`).
fn bubble_sort(values) {
    // Number of elements in the list.
    def n = len(values);

    // Outer loop: number of passes.
    def i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        // Inner loop: compare adjacent pairs, avoiding the already-sorted tail.
        def j = 0;
        while (j < n - 1 - i) {
            def left = heap_get(values, j);
            def right = heap_get(values, j + 1);

            // Swap if out of order.
            if (left > right) {
                def ignored1 = heap_set(values, j, right);
                def ignored2 = heap_set(values, j + 1, left);
            }

            j = j + 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }

    // Return the (now sorted) list pointer.
    return values;
}

// Print each list element on its own line.
fn print_list(values) {
    def i = 0;
    while (i < len(values)) {
        print(heap_get(values, i));
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Demo input: heap-backed list literal.
def numbers = new[5, 1, 4, 2, 8];

// Sort in-place; `sorted_numbers` points to the same heap list as `numbers`.
def sorted_numbers = bubble_sort(numbers);

// Print result.
def ignored3 = print_list(sorted_numbers);
